[FINISHED]Structs in Zig are composite data types that store a fixed set of named fields. They don't guarantee in-memory order or size. Structs are created using `T{}` syntax. Fields must be initialized, or an error occurs. Default values can be assigned to fields. Structs can contain functions and declarations. When accessing fields via a pointer, Zig automatically dereferences one level. For example, in a swap function within a struct, fields can be accessed directly without explicit dereferencing. This feature simplifies field manipulation within struct methods, enhancing code readability and maintainability.
